/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  membrane18_nf_feed_and_bleed  --  one NF module, a recycle loop, and the
  concentration the module actually works on.

  THE ARRANGEMENT.  A feed-and-bleed loop, the way a membrane skid is run
  when the recovery is set by recirculating the retentate: fresh feed joins
  the recycled RETENTATE, the mixture crosses the module once, the permeate
  leaves as product, and the retentate is split -- 95 % goes round again,
  the other 5 % is the BLEED (the concentrate).

      Feed --->[ MIX ]--->[ NF ]---> Permeate  (product)
                 ^           |
                 |        Retentate
                 |           |
                 +-- Recycle <--[ SPLIT ]--> Bleed  (concentrate)

  It is the pressure-driven analogue of ed07_feed_and_bleed: the same loop,
  the same trap.  A student sizes the module from the FEED analysis.  The
  module never sees the feed: it sees the MIXED loop concentration, and
  everything that depends on concentration is evaluated there.

  WHAT THE RUN MEASURES (printed by this run; nothing here is a target):

                                  loop (0.95/0.05)     single pass (0.0/1.0)
      glucose, fresh feed         9.970e-4 (mole fr.)   9.970e-4
      glucose, what the MODULE    1.804e-3   (1.81x)    9.970e-4
        is fed
      glucose, bleed              1.939e-3              2.641e-3
      glucose, permeate           5.614e-4              4.528e-4
      overall water recovery      68.45 %               75.29 %
      water flux (average)        165.2 LMH             182.5 LMH
      R_obs, module               68.54 %               54.36 %
      rejection of the PLANT,     43.7 %                54.6 %
        1 - x_perm / x_feed

  The single-pass column is this same case with `fractions ( 0.0  1.0 )`
  in SPLIT: nothing recycles, and the module is fed the fresh feed.

  THREE READINGS OF THE SAME LOOP.
    * The module works at 1.81 times the feed concentration, so its flux is
      165 LMH and not the 182 LMH the feed analysis gives: sizing from the
      feed undersizes the area by about 10 %.
    * R_obs = 68.54 % is the module's rejection AGAINST WHAT IT IS FED.  The
      permeate is 43.7 % cleaner than the fresh feed, not 68.5 %: a
      rejection is a property of the membrane at a concentration, and the
      plant is judged against the feed.  The single pass shows the opposite
      number (54.36 %), because there the concentration climbs ALONG the
      module and drags the average permeate up.
    * In THIS model the loop buys nothing.  It recovers less, runs at a
      lower flux and makes a dirtier permeate than one pass of the same
      module.  What a real loop buys is CROSS-FLOW -- a higher velocity
      along the membrane, hence a higher film coefficient and less
      polarisation and fouling -- and this model does not have it:
      `k_film` is DECLARED, 5.0e-5 m/s, whatever the recirculation flow.
      (membrane04 computes k_film and the pressure drop from the spacer
      hydraulics, which is what the recirculation would move; see WHY NO
      PUMP for why that is not used here.)  Read the numbers above as the
      cost of the loop, not as an argument against it.

  WHY NO PUMP, AND WHY dP_feed_total IS ZERO.  A real loop has a
  recirculation pump: the module loses feed-side pressure on every pass and
  the pump makes it up.  Here the mixer takes the LOWEST inlet pressure, so a
  loop whose recycle arrives a bar low and has no pump loses that bar again
  each pass and has no steady state.  The `pump` unit would restore it, and
  it now gets further than it did: glucose's record carries a liquid molar
  volume (Seitz et al. 2019, a partial molar volume at infinite dilution),
  which the incompressible pump model needs.  It still refuses, one datum
  later: the outlet temperature needs the liquid ENTHALPY of the mixture and
  glucose has no `liquidHeatCapacity` (a nonvolatile's enthalpy must not
  route through the ideal-gas reference).  The heat capacity of glucose in
  solution needs a primary source of its own, and a typed number would be a
  number with no source.  So the feed-side pressure drop is DECLARED ZERO,
  the loop sits at 10 bar throughout, and the pump's work is not counted
  anywhere.  Both are idealisations of this case, not of the module.

  THE ORDER OF THE UNITS IS THE ORDER THEY RUN.  `Recycle` is produced by the
  splitter (declared LAST) and consumed by the mixer (declared FIRST), so the
  plan cannot reach it without a cut: `system/solverDict` declares
  `tearStreams ( Recycle );` and a Newton on the tear variables closes it.
  Omit the declaration and the engine refuses by name with the cycle chain.

  WHAT IS PINNED AND WHAT IS NOT.  The golden pins the answer this model
  gives.  The feed is membrane02's 10 g/L glucose; the NF270 permeability set
  is the record's own pedagogical calibration to that case (it declares
  itself `fittedToCase`), so this is a structural witness of the loop and
  not a validation of NF270 on glucose.  No measured feed-and-bleed plant
  is reproduced anywhere in this tree; fouling is not modelled; and the
  energy balance is UNAVAILABLE, as it is for every glucose case, because
  the glucose record carries only its solid formation datum.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        MIX;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( Feed  Recycle );
        outputs     ( ModuleIn );

        //  ISOTHERMAL, declared: both streams are at 298.15 K and the loop is
        //  isothermal (nothing in the loop adds or removes heat; the pump's work is
        //  not modelled).  The mixed T is not in question.
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }

    {
        name        NF;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          ModuleIn;
        outputs     ( Retentate  Permeate );    // 0 = retentate, 1 = permeate

        operation
        {
            membrane         NF270;
            area             15.0;               // m2 (two NF270-4040 elements' worth, one module)
            length           1.0;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            k_film           5.0e-5;
            dP_feed_total    0.0 bar;             // see WHY NO PUMP
            nNodes           100;
        }
    }

    {
        name        SPLIT;
        type        splitter;
        in          Retentate;
        outputs     ( Recycle  Bleed );         // Recycle FEEDS BACK into MIX

        operation   { fractions ( 0.95  0.05 ); }
    }
);
